Z||000000||Rodrv6||11-23-1999||03:07 AM||||I have an '84 SE 2.5.  Does anyone know what the little sheetmetal part that resides in the intake manifold right under the throttle butterfly is for?  Over the years I have tried removing it and putting it back in to see if it makes any difference.  When the car was much newer it had a slight stumble coming off idle and removing the metal thingy seemed to help, but now I don't notice any difference whether it's in or out.  The off idle stumble finally went away about 6 or 7 years ago!  I was just curious.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Racer 1||11-23-1999||03:28 AM||||I think its that metal ramp deal right below the TBI gasket I was wondering that myself. I have no clue to what that is for.||||reg||5
Z||000002||Standard||11-23-1999||03:40 AM||||I think you're talking about the intake silencer/baffle, if I remember correctly. I remember seeing something about it in post awhile back (don't remember the topic).  It might be in there to smooth out the airflow?  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||William Federle||11-23-1999||08:16 AM||||I have a 1984 SE and I've had the TB off<BR>to put on a new gasket. I didn't see any<BR>metal thing below the butterfly. Is it <BR>important?||||reg||5
Z||000004||Rodrv6||11-23-1999||11:24 AM||||William, I drove around for a couple of years with mine removed and I couldn't tell any difference in the way the car ran or sounded.  I put it back in when the car wouldn't pass emissions (high HC at idle), but it didn't change a thing. I still don't have a clue as to it's purpose.||||reg||1
Z||000005||RZURCTN||11-23-1999||01:26 PM||||I think that it may be there to help airflow and to atomize the fuel more.  If you notice it slopes toward the front of the vehicle, I think this gets the fuel/air mixture moving in the right direction.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||11-23-1999||07:47 PM||||GM felt it helped inprove flow somehow. maybe by balancing flow to the middle cyls in some way.||||reg||1
